<p>{% person "george-peoples" %}George Peoples{% endperson %}, then a Bismarck resident, purchased lots 14 and 15 of Block 6 in Mandan from the Northern Pacific Railroad for {% inflation 375, 1881 %} in July 1881. He built a hotel in 1882–1883 for {% inflation 23500, 1882 %}, using brick manufactured just east of Mandan. Located at the northeastern corner of Main Street and Collins Avenue, it became the city's second three-story building, after the InterOcean Hotel four blocks west, and its first three-story brick building. The hotel opened in April 1882.</p>

<p>The walls were five bricks thick on the first floor, four on the second, and three on the third. Floor timbers measuring three inches by 12 inches came from a dismantled Minnesota flour mill. Bingenheimer and Taylor's Drug and Fancy Goods operated in the ground-floor storefront along Main Street, while S. Smith Dry Goods occupied the Stark Avenue, now Collins Avenue, side. Gussner's Meat Market stood east of the hotel and supplied its dining room. A second floor was later added to the market building, but an air shaft between the structures allowed the hotel windows to remain usable.</p>

<p>Peoples later sold the hotel to Pat McGinley, who renamed it the McGinley. Before air conditioning, families visited the hotel's dining room for home-cooked summer meals without having to heat their own houses.</p>

