<p>Jane Lauscher, widow of a successful Red River Valley bonanza farmer, purchased the hotel and operated it from approximately 1908 through 1916. She undertook a major remodeling of its rooms and marketed the hotel to railroad workers and area ranchers.</p>

<p>{% person "nels-hanson-romer" %}Nels{% endperson %} and Lillian Romer purchased the business in 1918. Nels served as a city commissioner in 1922–1923 and later became Mandan's police chief. Lillian continued operating the hotel briefly after he was shot and killed in the line of duty in December 1926.</p>

<p>In 1929, Otto Gussner opened a radiator shop behind the hotel, in space where the city had stored fire trucks until a new city hall was built. The shop moved to the rear of the lot along the alley when the hotel was remodeled in 1947.</p>

<p>The building operated exclusively as a hotel until 1968. Other tenants over the years included attorney John Sullivan, Taylor Drug, White Star Cafe, John Sand's Bar, St. Elmo's Restaurant in the basement, <em>The Mandan News</em>, and Crescent Printing. A Christian coffeehouse occupied the main floor for a decade before the final tenant, Ratz Lounge. The building was demolished in April and May 2007.</p>

<p>Sources included <em>Morton County Roots</em>, published in 1985; Susan Balcom's March 16, 2007, <em>Morton County Mandan News</em> article, "Mandan Hotel Has Seen Many Comings and Goings"; and an uncredited report about Mandan construction in the November 2, 1883, <em>Bismarck Weekly Tribune</em>.</p>
